{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"dates-intl","title":"Dates & Intl","group":"Collections & Iteration","order":40,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>Date</code> stores a single number — milliseconds since 1 Jan 1970 UTC — and has an awkward API. <code>Intl</code> formats dates, numbers and lists for any locale; <code>Temporal</code> is the modern replacement arriving now.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calendar-plus\"></i>Creating dates</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const epoch = new Date(0);\nconsole.log(epoch.toISOString());\nconst utc = new Date(Date.UTC(2024, 0, 31, 12, 30));   // months are 0-based!\nconsole.log(utc.toISOString(), utc.getTime());\nconsole.log(new Date(\"2024-02-29T10:00:00Z\").toISOString());\nconsole.log(new Date(\"not a date\").getTime(), String(new Date(\"nope\")));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1970-01-01T00:00:00.000Z\n2024-01-31T12:30:00.000Z 1706704200000\n2024-02-29T10:00:00.000Z\nNaN Invalid Date</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Months are <strong>0-based</strong> (<code>0</code> = January) but days are 1-based. Date-only strings like <code>\"2024-01-15\"</code> are parsed as <strong>UTC</strong>, while <code>\"2024-01-15T10:00\"</code> (no zone) is <strong>local</strong> time. Always store and send ISO strings with <code>Z</code> or an offset.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calculator\"></i>Reading and doing arithmetic</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const d = new Date(Date.UTC(2024, 0, 31));\nconsole.log(d.getUTCFullYear(), d.getUTCMonth(), d.getUTCDate(), d.getUTCDay());\nd.setUTCMonth(d.getUTCMonth() + 1);        // Jan 31 + 1 month → overflows!\nconsole.log(d.toISOString().slice(0, 10));\n\nconst a = new Date(\"2024-03-01T00:00:00Z\"), b = new Date(\"2024-03-15T00:00:00Z\");\nconst days = (b - a) / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24);   // subtraction → milliseconds\nconsole.log(days, a &lt; b);\nconst addDays = (date, n) =&gt; new Date(date.getTime() + n * 86_400_000);\nconsole.log(addDays(a, 10).toISOString());</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2024 0 31 3\n2024-03-02\n14 true\n2024-03-11T00:00:00.000Z</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>Date</code> objects are <strong>mutable</strong> — <code>setX</code> methods change them in place. Copy first: <code>new Date(d)</code>. Adding fixed milliseconds ignores daylight-saving shifts; for calendar maths use <code>Temporal</code> or a library (date-fns, Luxon, Day.js).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-world\"></i>Formatting with Intl</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const when = new Date(Date.UTC(2024, 6, 4, 15, 5));\nconst opts = { dateStyle: \"long\", timeStyle: \"short\", timeZone: \"UTC\" };\nconsole.log(new Intl.DateTimeFormat(\"en-US\", opts).format(when));\nconsole.log(new Intl.DateTimeFormat(\"en-GB\", opts).format(when));\nconsole.log(when.toLocaleDateString(\"en-IN\", { timeZone: \"Asia/Kolkata\", weekday: \"short\", day: \"numeric\", month: \"short\" }));\n\nconsole.log(new Intl.NumberFormat(\"en-IN\", { style: \"currency\", currency: \"INR\" }).format(1234567.5));\nconsole.log(new Intl.NumberFormat(\"en-US\", { notation: \"compact\" }).format(2_500_000));\nconsole.log(new Intl.NumberFormat(\"en-US\", { style: \"percent\" }).format(0.256));\nconst rtf = new Intl.RelativeTimeFormat(\"en\", { numeric: \"auto\" });\nconsole.log(rtf.format(-1, \"day\"), \"|\", rtf.format(3, \"week\"));\nconsole.log(new Intl.ListFormat(\"en\", { type: \"conjunction\" }).format([\"JS\", \"CSS\", \"HTML\"]));\nconsole.log(new Intl.PluralRules(\"en\", { type: \"ordinal\" }).select(22));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>July 4, 2024 at 3:05 PM\n4 July 2024 at 15:05\nThu, 4 Jul\n₹12,34,567.50\n2.5M\n26%\nyesterday | in 3 weeks\nJS, CSS, and HTML\ntwo</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock-hour-4\"></i>Temporal (the future of dates)</h3>\n<p><code>Temporal</code> (Stage 3, shipping in browsers from 2025–26) replaces <code>Date</code> with immutable, zone-aware types: <code>Temporal.PlainDate</code>, <code>PlainTime</code>, <code>ZonedDateTime</code>, <code>Instant</code>, <code>Duration</code>. Month numbers start at 1, and arithmetic like <code>date.add({ months: 1 })</code> is calendar-correct.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>// Temporal isn't enabled by default in Node 24 — shown for reference.\nconst d = Temporal.PlainDate.from(\"2024-01-31\");\nconsole.log(d.add({ months: 1 }).toString());   // 2024-02-29 (clamped, not overflowed)</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you compare two dates?</summary><div><p>Compare their timestamps: <code>a.getTime() === b.getTime()</code> for equality (<code>===</code> on Date objects compares references), and <code>&lt;</code>/<code>&gt;</code> or subtraction for ordering, since Dates coerce to their millisecond value.</p></div></details></section>"}